---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import UtilityHeader from '../../components/UtilityHeader.astro';

// Canonical dataset for this graphic (RM million; DOSM 2024). Mirrors
// infographics/tourism-state/WRITEUP.md and data.json.
const rows = [
  { state: 'Selangor', gdp: 485299, rec: 14226, dep: '2.9%' },
  { state: 'W.P. Kuala Lumpur*', gdp: 298334, rec: 14078, dep: '—' },
  { state: 'Johor', gdp: 187385, rec: 7814, dep: '4.2%' },
  { state: 'Sarawak', gdp: 184894, rec: 7957, dep: '4.3%' },
  { state: 'Pulau Pinang (Penang)', gdp: 136890, rec: 7424, dep: '5.4%' },
  { state: 'Sabah', gdp: 114530, rec: 8634, dep: '7.5%' },
  { state: 'Perak', gdp: 100202, rec: 7224, dep: '7.2%' },
  { state: 'Pahang', gdp: 82771, rec: 8710, dep: '10.5%' },
  { state: 'Negeri Sembilan', gdp: 66875, rec: 5869, dep: '8.8%' },
  { state: 'Kedah', gdp: 60466, rec: 4961, dep: '8.2%' },
  { state: 'Melaka', gdp: 57120, rec: 7933, dep: '13.9%' },
  { state: 'Terengganu', gdp: 39972, rec: 5170, dep: '12.9%' },
  { state: 'Kelantan', gdp: 32798, rec: 4541, dep: '13.8%' },
  { state: 'Perlis', gdp: 7330, rec: 1003, dep: '13.7%' },
  { state: 'W.P. Labuan', gdp: 8774, rec: 265, dep: '3.0%' },
  { state: 'W.P. Putrajaya*', gdp: null, rec: 937, dep: '—' },
];
const nf = (n: number | null) => (n == null ? '(in KL)' : n.toLocaleString('en-US'));

const card = 'background:var(--card);border:1px solid var(--border-light);border-radius:var(--radius-lg);padding:20px;margin-bottom:16px;';
const h2 = 'font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:var(--text-reading-lg);font-weight:700;color:var(--text-primary);margin:0 0 12px 0;letter-spacing:-0.03em;';
const p = 'font-family:var(--font-body);font-size:var(--text-body);line-height:1.72;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0 0 12px 0;';
---
<Base
  title="How tourism-dependent is each Malaysian state? — The Fourth Angle"
  description="Domestic tourism receipts as a share of state GDP, 2024. A data graphic from official DOSM statistics, with the full dataset, method, and sources."
>
  <UtilityHeader />
  <main style="max-width:760px;margin:0 auto;padding:24px 18px 80px;">
    <p style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:var(--text-xs);font-weight:600;color:var(--text-tertiary);margin:0 0 12px 0;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.1em;">Data graphic · Not an editorial issue</p>
    <h1 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:var(--text-title-lg);font-weight:700;color:var(--text-primary);margin:0 0 10px 0;letter-spacing:-0.04em;line-height:1.04;">How tourism-dependent is each Malaysian state?</h1>
    <p style="font-family:var(--font-body);font-size:var(--text-body);color:var(--text-tertiary);margin:0 0 24px 0;">Domestic tourism receipts as a share of state GDP, 2024.</p>

    <img
      src="/infographics/tourism-state.png"
      alt="Choropleth map of Malaysia shading each state by domestic tourism receipts as a percentage of state GDP in 2024, with a legend and two ranked panels."
      width="1240"
      height="1500"
      style="width:100%;height:auto;border:1px solid var(--border-light);border-radius:var(--radius-lg);margin-bottom:28px;"
    />

    <div style={card}>
      <h2 style={h2}>The one-line version</h2>
      <p style={p}>Tourism's weight in a state economy has little to do with how many ringgit it pulls in. <strong>Selangor earns the most domestic tourism money in the country — RM14.2 billion in 2024 — yet tourism is only 2.9% of its economy.</strong> In Melaka (13.9%), Kelantan (13.8%) and Perlis (13.7%), the same activity carries four to five times more economic weight, because the state economies underneath are small. Dependency is a ratio, and the denominator is the story.</p>
    </div>

    <div style={card}>
      <h2 style={h2}>What the metric is — and is not</h2>
      <p style={p}>For each state the graphic plots one derived ratio: <strong>domestic tourism receipts (2024) ÷ nominal state GDP (2024) × 100</strong>.</p>
      <p style={p}><strong>Domestic tourism only.</strong> Malaysia publishes international tourism receipts nationally, not by state (international arrivals are counted at the point of entry, not the destination). So this is not comparable to country-level "international tourism receipts as % of GDP" charts.</p>
      <p style={p}><strong>A derived ratio.</strong> DOSM does not publish this percentage; the numerator and denominator come from two different DOSM products. Treat it as indicative, not an official statistic.</p>
      <p style="font-family:var(--font-body);font-size:var(--text-body);line-height:1.72;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0;"><strong>Putrajaya is merged into Kuala Lumpur.</strong> DOSM reports Putrajaya's receipts (RM0.94b) separately but folds its GDP into W.P. Kuala Lumpur, so a standalone Putrajaya ratio is impossible from primary data. The KL tile combines both (RM15.02b receipts over KL GDP).</p>
    </div>

    <div style={card}>
      <h2 style={h2}>What the numbers say</h2>
      <p style={p}><strong>Most dependent</strong> (a big slice of a small economy): Melaka 13.9%, Kelantan 13.8%, Perlis 13.7%, Terengganu 12.9%, Pahang 10.5%.</p>
      <p style={p}><strong>Least dependent</strong> (large or resource-heavy economies): Selangor 2.9%, Labuan 3.0%, Johor 4.2%, Sarawak 4.3%.</p>
      <p style="font-family:var(--font-body);font-size:var(--text-body);line-height:1.72;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0;"><strong>Sabah vs Sarawak:</strong> similar absolute receipts (RM8.6b vs RM8.0b) but Sabah is far more dependent (7.5% vs 4.3%), because Sarawak's economy is larger and more resource-heavy.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:var(--text-reading-lg);font-weight:700;color:var(--text-primary);margin:8px 0 12px 0;letter-spacing:-0.03em;">The dataset</h2>
    <div style="overflow-x:auto;margin-bottom:8px;">
      <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-family:var(--font-body);font-size:var(--text-ui);">
        <thead>
          <tr style="border-bottom:2px solid var(--border-light);">
            <th style="text-align:left;padding:8px 6px;color:var(--text-tertiary);font-weight:700;">State</th>
            <th style="text-align:right;padding:8px 6px;color:var(--text-tertiary);font-weight:700;">GDP 2024 (RM m)</th>
            <th style="text-align:right;padding:8px 6px;color:var(--text-tertiary);font-weight:700;">Receipts 2024 (RM m)</th>
            <th style="text-align:right;padding:8px 6px;color:var(--text-tertiary);font-weight:700;">Dependency</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map((r) => (
            <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border-light);">
              <td style="text-align:left;padding:7px 6px;color:var(--text-primary);">{r.state}</td>
              <td style="text-align:right;padding:7px 6px;color:var(--text-secondary);">{nf(r.gdp)}</td>
              <td style="text-align:right;padding:7px 6px;color:var(--text-secondary);">{nf(r.rec)}</td>
              <td style="text-align:right;padding:7px 6px;color:var(--text-primary);font-weight:700;">{r.dep}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
          <tr style="border-top:2px solid var(--border-light);">
            <td style="text-align:left;padding:8px 6px;color:var(--text-primary);font-weight:700;">Malaysia</td>
            <td style="text-align:right;padding:8px 6px;color:var(--text-primary);font-weight:700;">1,932,292</td>
            <td style="text-align:right;padding:8px 6px;color:var(--text-primary);font-weight:700;">106,746</td>
            <td style="text-align:right;padding:8px 6px;color:var(--text-primary);font-weight:700;">5.5%</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p style="font-family:var(--font-body);font-size:var(--text-ui);line-height:1.6;color:var(--text-tertiary);margin:0 0 28px 0;">*The chart's <strong>Kuala Lumpur</strong> tile combines KL + Putrajaya receipts (15,015 RM m) over KL GDP → 5.0%. The Malaysia GDP total is DOSM's official national figure; it exceeds the state rows because it also includes a non-state "Supra" residual (RM68,652 m) not allocated to any state. <strong>Verification:</strong> the 16 per-state receipts sum to RM106,746 m ≈ RM106.7b — exactly DOSM's published national total.</p>

    <div style={card}>
      <h2 style={h2}>Method</h2>
      <p style={p}><strong>GDP (denominator).</strong> Nominal current-price GDP 2024, from the official GDP by State 2024 workbook, Table 43 "GDP by state at current prices", column 2024p.</p>
      <p style={p}><strong>Receipts (numerator).</strong> Each state's Domestic Tourism Survey 2024 report, "Jumlah Terimaan / Total Receipts (RM million)", 2024 column — cross-read against each report's prose and reconciled to the RM106.7b national total.</p>
      <p style="font-family:var(--font-body);font-size:var(--text-body);line-height:1.72;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0;"><strong>Map.</strong> True geographic choropleth from DOSM's open administrative_1_state boundary file. Every value is flagged verified (transcribed from a DOSM primary release).</p>
    </div>

    <div style="background:var(--card);border:1px solid var(--border-light);border-radius:var(--radius-lg);padding:20px;margin-bottom:16px;">
      <h2 style={h2}>Sources</h2>
      <p style={p}>All data: <strong>Department of Statistics Malaysia (DOSM)</strong>, CC-BY 4.0. Attribute DOSM.</p>
      <ul style="font-family:var(--font-body);font-size:var(--text-ui);line-height:1.7;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0;padding-left:18px;">
        <li><strong>GDP by State, 2024</strong> — Table 43, current prices, column 2024p. <a href="https://open.dosm.gov.my/publications/gdp_state_2024" style="color:var(--accent,#3b3b76);">Publication</a> · <a href="https://www.dosm.gov.my/portal-main/release-content/gross-domestic-product-gdp-by-state-2024" style="color:var(--accent,#3b3b76);">Release</a></li>
        <li><strong>Domestic Tourism Survey (States), 2024</strong> — 16 per-state reports, "Jumlah Terimaan" 2024 column. <a href="https://www.dosm.gov.my/portal-main/release-content/domestic-tourism-survey-states-2024" style="color:var(--accent,#3b3b76);">Release</a></li>
        <li><strong>Domestic Tourism Survey, 2024</strong> (annual) — national total used to reconcile. <a href="https://open.dosm.gov.my/publications/tourism_domestic_annual_2024" style="color:var(--accent,#3b3b76);">Publication</a></li>
        <li><strong>State boundaries</strong> — DOSM open data, administrative_1_state. <a href="https://open.dosm.gov.my" style="color:var(--accent,#3b3b76);">open.dosm.gov.my</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p style="font-family:var(--font-body);font-size:var(--text-ui);line-height:1.6;color:var(--text-tertiary);margin:24px 0 0 0;font-style:italic;">Compiled by The Fourth Angle. Figures are official DOSM statistics; the dependency ratio is a derived presentation, not an official DOSM figure.</p>
  </main>
</Base>
